# Citation File Format — https://citation-file-format.github.io
#
# rust-MAFFT is a pure-Rust port of MAFFT, the multiple sequence alignment
# software developed by Kazutaka Katoh and colleagues at the Computational
# Biology Research Center (CBRC). The scientific contribution — the FFT-
# anchored alignment algorithm, the iterative-refinement variants, the
# scoring matrices — is theirs. This port re-engineers that work in Rust
# while preserving byte-identical output.
#
# If you use rust-MAFFT in published work you MUST cite the original
# MAFFT paper (Katoh & Standley 2013). The rust-MAFFT manuscript (Goiriz,
# in preparation, Bioinformatics) describes the port and should be cited
# alongside once published; until then, please cite Katoh & Standley.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use rust-MAFFT in scientific work, please cite the original
  MAFFT paper (Katoh & Standley 2013) — the scientific contribution is
  theirs. Cite the rust-MAFFT manuscript additionally once it is
  published.
title: rust-MAFFT
abstract: >-
  Pure-Rust port of MAFFT 7.526 (Katoh & Standley 2013) producing
  byte-identical output across the BAliBASE 3 benchmark (1930/1930)
  for every supported alignment mode. Distributed as a Rust library
  (`mafft`, `mafft-core`), a standalone CLI (`mafft-rs`), and Python
  bindings (`pymafft`).
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Goiriz
    given-names: Lucas
    email: lucas@spearbit.com
    # ORCID: add when ready

version: "0.1.0"
date-released: "2026-06-05"
license: "MIT AND BSD-3-Clause"
repository-code: "https://github.com/luksgrin/rust-MAFFT"
url: "https://luksgrin.github.io/rust-MAFFT/"
keywords:
  - bioinformatics
  - multiple-sequence-alignment
  - msa
  - mafft
  - rust
  - port

# Preferred citation: the canonical MAFFT v7 paper that rust-MAFFT
# implements. Update this once the rust-MAFFT manuscript is published —
# at that point, the rust-MAFFT paper goes here and Katoh & Standley
# 2013 moves into `references` (it stays required, just no longer
# preferred-by-default).
preferred-citation:
  type: article
  title: "MAFFT multiple sequence alignment software version 7: improvements in performance and usability"
  authors:
    - family-names: Katoh
      given-names: Kazutaka
    - family-names: Standley
      given-names: Daron M.
  journal: "Molecular Biology and Evolution"
  volume: 30
  issue: 4
  start: 772
  end: 780
  year: 2013
  month: 4
  doi: "10.1093/molbev/mst010"
  url: "https://academic.oup.com/mbe/article/30/4/772/1073398"
  publisher:
    name: Oxford University Press
  issn: "1537-1719"

references:
  - type: article
    title: "MAFFT: a novel method for rapid multiple sequence alignment based on fast Fourier transform"
    authors:
      - family-names: Katoh
        given-names: Kazutaka
      - family-names: Misawa
        given-names: Kazuharu
      - family-names: Kuma
        given-names: Kei-ichi
      - family-names: Miyata
        given-names: Takashi
    journal: "Nucleic Acids Research"
    volume: 30
    issue: 14
    start: 3059
    end: 3066
    year: 2002
    month: 7
    doi: "10.1093/nar/gkf436"
    url: "https://academic.oup.com/nar/article/30/14/3059/2904316"
    publisher:
      name: Oxford University Press
    issn: "1362-4962"
    notes: >-
      The foundational MAFFT paper introducing the FFT-anchored
      approach to multiple sequence alignment. Cite if the FFT-NS-1/2
      modes are relevant to your work.

  - type: article
    title: "MAFFT version 5: improvement in accuracy of multiple sequence alignment"
    authors:
      - family-names: Katoh
        given-names: Kazutaka
      - family-names: Kuma
        given-names: Kei-ichi
      - family-names: Toh
        given-names: Hiroyuki
      - family-names: Miyata
        given-names: Takashi
    journal: "Nucleic Acids Research"
    volume: 33
    issue: 2
    start: 511
    end: 518
    year: 2005
    doi: "10.1093/nar/gki198"
    url: "https://academic.oup.com/nar/article/33/2/511/2401127"
    publisher:
      name: Oxford University Press
    notes: >-
      MAFFT v5: introduction of iterative refinement, the BLOSUM
      matrix integration, and the L-INS-i / G-INS-i / E-INS-i
      strategies. Cite if you use any of the iterative modes.

  - type: article
    title: "PartTree: an algorithm to build an approximate tree from a large number of unaligned sequences"
    authors:
      - family-names: Katoh
        given-names: Kazutaka
      - family-names: Toh
        given-names: Hiroyuki
    journal: "Bioinformatics"
    volume: 23
    issue: 3
    start: 372
    end: 374
    year: 2007
    doi: "10.1093/bioinformatics/btl592"
    publisher:
      name: Oxford University Press
    notes: >-
      The PartTree guide-tree construction used by --parttree /
      --dpparttree for large datasets (10K+ sequences). Cite if you
      use --parttree.
